Proposed replacement for lines 82–90:

```python
for message in stream.messages:
    thinking_open = False
    for event in message:
        if event.get("event") != "content-block-delta":
            continue
        delta = event.get("delta")
        if not isinstance(delta, dict):
            continue

        if delta.get("type") == "reasoning-delta":
            if not DISPLAY_THINKING:
                continue
            if not thinking_open:
                print("<THINKING>", end="", flush=True)
                thinking_open = True
            print(delta.get("reasoning", ""), end="", flush=True)

        elif delta.get("type") == "text-delta":
            if thinking_open:
                print("</THINKING>\n", end="", flush=True)
                thinking_open = False
            print(delta.get("text", ""), end="", flush=True)

    if thinking_open:
        print("</THINKING>\n", end="", flush=True)
```

Why this fixes it: there's now exactly one consumer per message — the raw event iterator — instead of two competing views of it. Every event is handled the instant it's pumped, so reasoning and text both print live, in the order the model actually produced them, and the transition between `<THINKING>` and the answer happens at the real boundary (the first `text-delta`) instead of after an artificial full drain. `thinking_open` is just bookkeeping so the wrapper tags open/close correctly regardless of how many reasoning bursts happen before text starts (some models interleave more than one).

One behavior change worth flagging: if `DISPLAY_THINKING` is off, reasoning events are still pumped (skipped via `continue`, not avoided) — they have to be, since skipping the pump entirely would stall the text side too. That's unavoidable with this API; you're not paying for them twice, just not printing them.

